Chapter 11 — Part 1
Curtis Blinked.
Landslide zone—cleared. Ruins zone—Kirishima and Bakugo holding a choke point, explosions lighting the dust. Downpour zone—Todoroki’s ice already locking half the villains in place. Mountain zone—Tokoyami and Shoji moving through shadow. Every pocket of Class 1-A was still fighting. Valiant. Bloody in places. Still standing.
He hit the edge of the central plaza and stopped.
Aizawa was on one knee, elbow ruined, capture weapon slack. Shigaraki stood over him, fingers flexing, Decay still eating at the air. The massive black shape of Nomu loomed behind the ringleader, exposed brain pulsing, yellow eyes empty. Thirteen lay farther up the stairs, suit ruptured, out of the fight. Kurogiri’s mist sealed the main exit. Iida was already gone—engines screaming toward the doors Shoji had forced open. The rest of the class was still scattered or pinned.
Midoriya, Tsuyu, and Minoru reached the plaza steps at the same time Curtis did. They froze at the sight of their teacher on the ground.
Curtis began to shake.
It started in his hands. Then his shoulders. The antlers trembled. Both eye slits narrowed to burning lines—red and blue locked on Shigaraki and the thing beside him. The low, steady pull in his chest didn’t feel steady anymore. It sat hot. Tight. The kind of heat that came before something in the mountains decided the talking was over.
Rage.
His voice came out low, filtered, and nothing like the even tone from the bus.
“Get behind me.”
He stepped forward. The plaza concrete cracked under his boots. Nomu’s head turned. Shigaraki’s scratching paused. Curtis didn’t stop.
Curtis lunged off the steps so hard the concrete shattered under his boots.
“Get the hell away from him!”
The distance vanished. His fist drove forward, aimed straight at Shigaraki’s chest. The ringleader twisted at the last second, coat snapping, fingers missing Curtis’s arm by inches. Kurogiri didn’t bother dodging with a body—he simply ceased to be solid. The punch cratered the plaza floor where both of them had been, shockwave kicking up dust and broken tile.
Curtis didn’t reset. He Blinked.
The world folded. One heartbeat he stood in the new crater. The next he was on the stairs, armored arms sliding under Thirteen’s ruined suit and lifting her clear of the steps in one careful motion. Another Blink and he was back beside Midoriya, Tsuyu, and Minoru, the rescue hero held secure against his chest. Tsuyu had already dragged Aizawa the last few meters with her tongue; both teachers were now behind the small group.
He lowered Thirteen onto the step as gently as the armor allowed. The deer-skull mask turned toward the three students. Red and blue slits burned.
“Keep them safe.” His voice stayed low, filtered, steady despite the shake still sitting in his shoulders. “I’ll hold these two off for as long as I can. Don’t come back down here.”
Midoriya’s hands hovered, torn. “Beardy—Curtis—you can’t—”
“I can.” Curtis straightened. Water and dust slid off the cloak. “Get them to the exit the second Iida clears it. That’s the job.”
Down in the plaza Shigaraki rolled his neck, scratching idly at the skin under his collar. “Persistent. Kurogiri, the big one stays. I want to see what the faculty pet does when the children aren’t behind him.”
Nomu’s yellow eyes tracked Curtis. The massive body shifted, ready.
Curtis rolled his shoulders once, bones cracking under the armor. He stepped back onto the open plaza alone, boots grinding debris, and set his feet.
“Go,” he said again, without looking back.
Then he faced the three villains and waited for the first move.
Curtis lunged for Shigaraki.
Nomu met him halfway.
The bio-engineered monster crossed the crater in a single bound, massive arm swinging. Curtis’s fist crashed into Nomu’s forearm instead of the ringleader. The impact detonated—bone-deep, deafening. Shockwaves ripped outward, shattering what was left of the nearby plaza tiles. Both of them held.
Nomu’s yellow eyes didn’t blink. Curtis’s mask didn’t shift. They traded again. Curtis drove a left into the thing’s ribs. Nomu answered with a backhand that rang off the antlers and staggered him half a step. Another punch. Another. Each collision louder than the last, the sound bouncing off the dome like thunder.
Shigaraki watched from a few meters back, fingers still, head tilted. Kurogiri hovered at his shoulder. Neither interfered.
Curtis planted and swung harder. Nomu caught the wrist, twisted, and slammed a knee toward his midsection. Curtis Blinked out of the grip, reappeared at the monster’s flank, and hammered three rapid shots into the exposed side before Nomu’s regeneration even started. The return elbow caught him across the chest plate and sent him skidding.
He dug in, boots carving trenches, and charged again. The next exchange of punches cracked the air so hard the students on the stairs flinched. Dust and debris rained. Neither fighter gave ground.
The low heat in Curtis’s chest burned cold and focused. Nomu’s regeneration knit every dent almost as fast as it formed. Curtis kept punching anyway.
Aizawa coughed hard against the step, blood flecking the capture weapon still wrapped around his neck. His ruined elbow shook as he planted his good hand and tried to push upright.
“I can’t let him fight them on his own.” Another wet cough tore out of him. “Cough—cough—damn it.”
Tsuyu’s tongue tightened gently around his torso, holding him down. “Mr. Aizawa, you’re injured. Ribbit. You need to stay—”
“He’s a student.” Aizawa’s visible eye stayed locked on the plaza, where another thunderous exchange of punches shook the dome. “Doesn’t matter what Nezu cleared. He’s still—” He broke off, coughing again, body trembling with the effort to rise. “Still mine to keep alive.”
Midoriya hovered, torn between the order Curtis had given and the sight of their teacher struggling. Minoru had gone pale. Thirteen remained unconscious beside them.
Aizawa’s arm buckled. He hit the step again, breathing ragged, but his eye never left the fight. “If he starts losing… you run. All of you. That’s not a suggestion.”
The plaza boomed again. Curtis and Nomu were still trading blows, neither yielding. Aizawa’s jaw clenched through the pain, and he kept trying to get his feet under him anyway.
Shigaraki moved.
He didn’t announce it. One second he stood at the edge of the crater. The next his hand flashed toward Curtis’s shoulder, Decay already eating the air. Curtis twisted, the touch grazing armor instead of skin. The plate crumbled to dust on contact. Nomu capitalized—massive fist crashing into Curtis’s ribs and driving him back three steps.
Two on one.
Curtis set his feet again, mask level, both eyes burning. He slipped another Decay swipe, answered with a short punch that staggered Nomu, then had to Blink clear when Shigaraki’s fingers came for his throat. The rhythm broke. Every exchange cost more.
This is getting bad. The thought cut cold through the impacts. If this keeps up, I’ll lose. And I’ll die. Another block. Another crater. I’m sorry, Cathleen. I’m sorry, Mr. Principal. If I die, know I kept my promise. I kept the quirk a secret.
He drove a knee into Nomu’s midsection and immediately had to abandon the follow-up when Shigaraki closed again. Regeneration knit the damage. Decay waited for the smallest opening. The gap was shrinking.
On the stairs Aizawa forced himself higher, blood on his teeth, voice ripping out of a ruined throat.
“PLUS ULTRA! YOUNG BEARDY! IF YOU DIE—YOU’RE EXPELLED!”
The yell cracked across the plaza. Curtis’s head snapped toward the sound for half a second. The blue eye flared brighter. He turned back into the two villains, cloak shredded, armor failing in pieces, and met the next charge head-on.
